# Read the number of integers (N), which is not strictly necessary for the logic,
# but the input format requires reading it.
N = int(input())

# Read the space-separated list of integers and store them as a list of strings.
# This makes it easier to check for the palindrome property later.
integers_str = input().split()

# Condition 1: All integers must be positive.
# We use the all() function with a generator expression to check if int(x) > 0 for all x.
all_positive = all(int(x) > 0 for x in integers_str)

# Condition 2: Check if any integer is a palindromic integer.
# We use the any() function with a generator expression to check if any x is a palindrome.
# A string is a palindrome if it reads the same forwards and backwards (x == x[::-1]).
any_palindrome = any(x == x[::-1] for x in integers_str)

# The final output should be True only if both conditions are satisfied.
print(all_positive and any_palindrome)

